          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theory"} generalized cohomology theory
            which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} represented
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+sphere+spectrum"} equivariant sphere spectrum
            may also be called
            = succeed "," do
              %em equivariant stable cohomotopy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+stable+homotopy+theory"} equivariant stable homotopy theory
            version of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+cohomotopy"} stable cohomotopy
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomotopy"} cohomotopy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goodwillie-Taylor+tower"} Goodwillie approximation
            of plain (“unstable”)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomotopy"} equivariant cohomotopy
          %p
            Just as the plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere+spectrum"} sphere spectrum
            is a distinguished object of plain
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+sphere+spectrum"} equivariant sphere spectrum
            is distinguished in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+stable+homotopy+theory"} equivariant stable homotopy theory
            and hence so is equivariant stable cohomotopy theory.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#AsAlgebraicKTheoryOverTheFieldWithOneElement
            As equivariant algebraic K-theory over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_1
          %p
            The following is known as the
            = succeed ":" do
              %em Barratt-Priddy-Quillen theorem
          #StableCohomotopyIsKTheoryOfFinSet.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+cohomotopy"} stable cohomotopy
                is K-theory of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinSet"} FinSet
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C} =
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinSet"} FinSet
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeleton"} skeleton
              of the category of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+sets"} finite sets
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutative+category"} permutative category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+of+a+permutative+category"} K-theory of this permutative category
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi FinSet
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≃
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi 𝕊
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    K(FinSet) \;\simeq\; \mathbb{S}
            %p
              is represented by the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere+spectrum"} sphere spectrum
          %p
            This is due to
            %a{:href => "#BarrattPriddy72"} Barratt-Priddy 72
            reproved in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Segal74"} Segal 74, Prop. 3.5
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Priddy73"} Priddy 73
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Glasman13"} Glasman 13
          #StableCohomotopyIsAlgebraicKTheoryOverFieldWithOneElement.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+cohomotopy"} stable cohomotopy
                as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} algebraic K-theory
                over the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+with+one+element"} field with one element
            %p
              Notice that for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+of+a+permutative+category"} K-theory of a permutative category
              of its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+modules"} category of modules
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F Mod
              is its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} algebraic K-theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K F
              (see
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "K-theory+of+a+permutative+category#OrdinaryAlgebraicKTheoryFromPermutativeCategoryOfProjectiveModules"} this example
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mi F
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≃
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mi Mod
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    K F \;\simeq\; K(F Mod)
                    \\,.
            %p
              Now (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+sets"}> pointed
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+sets"} finite sets
